      "name": "dated_workshop_schedule_and_FPI_context",
      "text": "ICLR 2025 Workshop on Trustworthy LLM (https://building-trust-in-llms.github.io/iclr-workshop/schedule.html)\nciteturn6901view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6900view1\",\"id\":3}); Total lines: 26\nL0: # ICLR 2025 Workshop\nL1: Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications:\nL2: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\nL3: ## Location: Hall 4 #6\nL4: Date: 28 Apr 2025 (Monday)\nL5: \nL6: cite0†HOME cite1†SPEAKERS AND PANELISTS cite2†CALL FOR PAPERS cite3†WORKSHOP SCHEDULE L7: \nL8: Schedule:\nL9: Time  | Session\nL10: --- | ---\nL11: 8:50am-9:00am  | Introduction and Opening Remarks\nL12: 9:00am-9:40am  | Invited Talk 1 - Reza Shokri\nL13: 9:40am-10:20am  | Invited Talk 2 - Yoshua Bengio\nL14: 10:20am-10:35am  | Break\nL15: 10:35am-11:15am  | Invited Talk 3 - Shayne Longpre\nL16: 11:15am-12:15pm  | cite4†Poster Session 1 (46 papers) L17: 12:15pm-1:30pm  | Lunch Break\nL18: 1:30pm-2:10pm  | Invited Talk 4 - Edoardo Debenedetti\nL19: 2:10pm-2:50pm  | cite5†Oral Presentations 1 (4 papers – 10 min each) L20: 2:50pm-3:25pm  | Panel Discussion: Yarin Gal, Ashwinee Panda, Zack Lipton\nL21: 3:25pm-3:40pm  | Break\nL22: 3:40pm-4:20pm  | Invited Talk 5 - Jonas Geiping\nL23: 4:20pm-5:00pm  | cite6†Oral Presentation 2 (4 papers – 10 min each) L24: 5:00pm-6:00pm  | cite7†Poster Session 2 (46 papers) L25: ICLR 2025 Workshop - Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFPI @ ICLR2025 (https://sites.google.com/view/fpiworkshop/about)\nciteturn6901view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6900view2\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 203\nL0: [Input]\nL1: \nL2: Search this site\nL3: \nL4: Embedded Files\nL5: \nL6: Skip to main content\nL7: \nL8: Skip to navigation\nL9: \nL10: cite0†FPI @ ICLR2025 L11: \nL12: # Frontiers in Probabilistic Inference:\nL13: Sampling M eets Learning\nL14: \nL15: ### April 2 8th @ ICLR 2025, Singapore\nL16: ##\nL17: \nL18: About the Workshop\nL19: The F rontiers in P robabilistic I nference: Sampling meets Learning (FPI) workshop at ICLR 2025 focuses on modern approaches to probabilistic inference to address the challenging and under-explored area of sampling from an unnormalized distribution. Sampling spans a wide range of difficult and timely problems from molecular dynamics simulation, and Bayesian posterior inference/inverse problems to sampling from generative models weighted by target density (e.g. finetuning, inference-time alignment).\nL20: We hope to provide an inclusive and collaborative environment to discuss emerging ML methods for learning samplers and their applications to real-world problems. We aim to facilitate discussions around identifying some key challenges of learning-based approaches, compared to classical sampling approaches, along with techniques to overcome them.\nL21: We will center workshop discussions around the following topics/questions:\nL22: \nL23:   * Sampling methods and their connections to optimal transport and optimal control.\nL24: \nL25:   * Classical sampling approaches and how learning accelerates them.\nL26: \nL27:   * Connections between sampling methods and physics.\nL28: \nL29:   * Understanding sampling from theoretical perspectives.\nL30: \nL31:   * Applications of sampling to natural sciences, Bayesian inference, LLM fine-tuning, and more.\nL32: \nL33: Submissions:\nL34:   * See the cite1†Call for Papers page for in formation about our submission tracks and instructions on how to submit a paper on cite2†OpenReview†openreview.net .\nL35: \nL36:   * All accepted submissions will be invited to present a poster at the workshop (in person). We will invite some of the accepted submissions to give a spotlight talk at the workshop.\nL37: ##\nL38: \nL39: Confirmed Speakers\nL40: \nL41: ###\nL42: \nL43: cite3†Michael Albergo†malbergo.me L44: \nL45: Harvard University, I AIFI\nL46: \nL47: ###\nL48: \nL49: cite4†Sitan Chen†sitanchen.com L50: \nL51: Harvard University\nL52: \nL53: ###\nL54: \nL55: cite5†Marylou Gabrié†marylou-gabrie.github.io L56: \nL57: École Normale Supérieure\nL58: \nL59: ###\nL60: \nL61: cite6†Emtiyaz Khan†emtiyaz.github.io L62: \nL63: Advanced Intelligence Project, Tokyo\nL64: \nL65: ###\nL66: \nL67: cite7†Grant M. Rotskoff†chemistry.stanford.edu L68: \nL69: Stanford University\nL70: \nL71: ###\nL72: \nL73: cite8†Francisco Vargas†www.cst.cam.ac.uk L74: \nL75: Xaira Therapeutics, Cambridge University\nL76: \nL77: ##\nL78: \nL79: Confirmed Panelists\nL80: ###\nL81: \nL82: cite9†Ricky T. Q. Chen†rtqichen.github.io L83: \nL84: FAIR, Meta\nL85: \nL86: ###\nL87: \nL88: Sinho Chewi\nL89: \nL90: Yale University\nL91: \nL92: ###\nL93: \nL94: cite10†Michael Hutchinson†mjhutchinson.info L95: \nL96: Isomorphic Labs\nL97: \nL98: ###\nL99: \nL100: cite11†Pranav Murugan†pranavmurugan.github.io L101: \nL102: Genesis Therapeutics\nL103: \nL104: ###\nL105: \nL106: cite12†Molei Tao†mtao8.math.gatech.edu L107: \nL108: Georgia Tech\nL109: \nL110: ##\nL111: \nL112: Organizers\nL113: \nL114: ###\nL115: \nL116: cite13†TaraAkhound-Sadegh L117: \nL118: Mila, McGill University\nL119: \nL120: ###\nL121: \nL122: cite14†MartaSkreta †martaskreta.notion.site L123: \nL124: University of Toronto, Vector Institute\nL125: ###\nL126: \nL127: cite15†YuanqiDu †yuanqidu.github.io L128: \nL129: Cornell University\nL130: \nL131: ###\nL132: \nL133: cite16†Sarthak Mittal†sarthmit.github.io L134: \nL135: Mila, Université de Montréal , Meta\nL136: \nL137: ###\nL138: \nL139: cite17†JoeyBose†joeybose.github.io L140: \nL141: Oxford University, Mila\nL142: \nL143: ###\nL144: \nL145: cite18†AlexTong†www.alextong.net L146: \nL147: Mila, Université de Montréal\nL148: \nL149: ###\nL150: \nL151: cite19†KirillNeklyudov†necludov.github.io L152: \nL153: Mila, Université de Montréal\nL154: \nL155: ###\nL156: \nL157: cite20†MichaelBronstein†www.cs.ox.ac.uk L158: \nL159: Oxford University, Aithyra, Deepmind\nL160: ###\nL161: \nL162: cite21†MaxWelling†staff.fnwi.uva.nl L163: \nL164: University of\nL165: Amsterdam, CuspAI\nL166: \nL167: ###\nL168: \nL169: cite22†ArnaudDoucet†www.stats.ox.ac.uk L170: \nL171: Google Deepmind\nL172: \nL173: ###\nL174: \nL175: cite23†AapoHyvärinen†www.cs.helsinki.fi L176: \nL177: University of Helsinki\nL178: \nL179: ##\nL180: \nL181: Our Sponsors\nL182: \nL183: cite24†Image L184: \nL185: ##\nL186: \nL187: Workshop Venue\nL188: \nL189: Singapore EXPO\nL190: \nL191: 1 Expo Dr, Singapore 486150\nL192: \nL193: Contact\nL194: fpi-workshop@googlegroups.com\nL195: \nL196: If you have any questions, please contact us at fpi-workshop@googlegroups.com\nL197: \nL198: Page updated\nL199: \nL200: Google Sites\nL201: \nL202: Report abuse\n"
