      "name": "exact_Aegis_poster_session_1_and_two_orals",
      "text": "Poster Session 1 - ICLR 2025 Workshop on Trustworthy LLM (https://building-trust-in-llms.github.io/iclr-workshop/poster_session_1.html)\nciteturn6904view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6901view0\",\"id\":4}); Total lines: 99\nL0: # ICLR 2025 Workshop\nL1: Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications:\nL2: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\nL3: \nL4: cite0†HOME cite1†SPEAKERS AND PANELISTS cite2†CALL FOR PAPERS cite3†WORKSHOP SCHEDULE L5: ## Poster Session 1 (11:15am-12:15pm)\nL6: \nL7: AdvBDGen: A Robust Framework for Generating Adaptive and Stealthy Backdoors in LLM Alignment Attacks\nL8: \nL9: Interpretable Steering of Large Language Models with Feature Guided Activation Additions\nL10: \nL11: The Differences Between Direct Alignment Algorithms are a Blur\nL12: \nL13: Towards Effective Discrimination Testing for Generative AI\nL14: \nL15: Scalable Fingerprinting of Large Language Models\nL16: \nL17: Prune 'n Predict: Optimizing LLM Decision-making with Conformal Prediction\nL18: SPEX: Scaling Feature Interaction Explanations for LLMs\nL19: \nL20: Towards Understanding Fine-Tuning Mechanisms of LLMs via Circuit Analysis\nL21: \nL22: Differentially Private Retrieval Augmented Generation with Random Projection\nL23: \nL24: LLMS LOST IN TRANSLATION: M-ALERT UNCOVERS CROSS-LINGUISTIC SAFETY GAPS\nL25: \nL26: Unnatural Languages Are Not Bugs but Features for LLMs\nL27: \nL28: Working Memory Attack on LLMs\nL29: \nL30: No, Of Course I Can! Refusal Mechanisms Can Be Exploited Using Harmless Data\nL31: VideoJail: Exploiting Video-Modality Vulnerabilities for Jailbreak Attacks on Multimodal Large Language Models\nL32: \nL33: Analyzing Memorization in Large Language Models through the Lens of Model Attribution\nL34: \nL35: PRUNING AS A DEFENSE: REDUCING MEMORIZATION IN LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS\nL36: \nL37: Antipodal Pairing and Mechanistic Signals in Dense SAE Latents\nL38: \nL39: Evaluating Text Humanlikeness via Self-Similarity Exponent\nL40: \nL41: Self-Ablating Transformers: More Interpretability, Less Sparsity\nL42: Justified Trust in AI Fairness Assessment using Existing Metadata Entities\nL43: \nL44: Disentangling Linguistic Features with Dimension-Wise Analysis of Vector Embeddings\nL45: \nL46: Fast Proxies for LLM Robustness Evaluation\nL47: \nL48: A False Sense of Privacy: Evaluating Textual Data Sanitization Beyond Surface-level Privacy Leakage\nL49: \nL50: Rethinking LLM Bias Probing Using Lessons from the Social Sciences\nL51: \nL52: AI Companions Are Not The Solution To Loneliness: Design Choices And Their Drawbacks\nL53: A Generative Approach to LLM Harmfulness Detection with Red Flag Tokens\nL54: \nL55: A Missing Testbed for LLM Pre-Training Membership Inference Attacks\nL56: \nL57: Harmful Helper: Perform malicious tasks? Web AI agents might help\nL58: \nL59: Mechanistic Anomaly Detection for \"Quirky'' Language Models\nL60: \nL61: Systematic Evaluation of LLM-as-a-Judge in LLM Alignment Tasks: Explainable Metrics and Diverse Prompt Templates\nL62: \nL63: Latent Adversarial Training Improves the Representation of Refusal\nL64: \nL65: Reliable and Efficient Amortized Model-based Evaluation\nL66: SafeMERGE: Preserving Safety Alignment in Fine-Tuned Large Language Models via Selective Layer-Wise Model Merging\nL67: \nL68: Top of the CLASS: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Real-World Enterprise Tasks\nL69: \nL70: Evaluating and Mitigating the Safety Awareness-Execution Gaps of LM Agents\nL71: \nL72: Diagnostic Uncertainty: Teaching Language Models to Describe Open-Ended Uncertainty\nL73: \nL74: AegisLLM: Scaling Agentic Systems for Self-Reflective Defense in LLM Security\nL75: \nL76: Steering Fine-Tuning Generalization with Targeted Concept Ablation\nL77: PATTERNS AND MECHANISMS OF CONTRASTIVE ACTIVATION ENGINEERING\nL78: \nL79: Hidden No More: Attack and Defending Private Third-Party LLM Inference\nL80: \nL81: MKA: Leveraging Cross-Lingual Consensus for Model Abstention\nL82: \nL83: Finding Sparse Autoencoder Representations Of Errors In CoT Prompting\nL84: \nL85: A Flexible Large Language Models Guardrail Development Methodology Applied to Off-Topic Prompt Detection\nL86: \nL87: Can Knowledge Graphs Make Large Language Models More Trustworthy? An Empirical Study Over Open-ended Question Answering\nL88: FiDeLiS: Faithful Reasoning in Large Language Models for Knowledge Graph Question Answering\nL89: \nL90: Mind the Gap: A Practical Attack on GGUF Quantization\nL91: \nL92: On-Premises LLM Deployment Demands a Middle Path: Preserving Privacy Without Sacrificing Model\nL93: \nL94: In-Context Meta Learning Induces Multi-Phase Circuit Emergence\nL95: \nL96: GASP: Efficient Black-Box Generation of Adversarial Suffixes for Jailbreaking LLMs\nL97: \nL98: ICLR 2025 Workshop - Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOral Presentations 1 - ICLR 2025 Workshop on Trustworthy LLM (https://building-trust-in-llms.github.io/iclr-workshop/oral_presentations_1.html)\nciteturn6904view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6901view0\",\"id\":5}); Total lines: 24\nL0: # ICLR 2025 Workshop\nL1: Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications:\nL2: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\nL3: \nL4: cite0†HOME cite1†SPEAKERS AND PANELISTS cite2†CALL FOR PAPERS cite3†WORKSHOP SCHEDULE L5: \nL6: ## Oral Presentations 1 (2:10pm-2:50pm)\nL7: \nL8: Paper 12\nL9: \nL10: ### Mind the Gap: A Practical Attack on GGUF Quantization\nL11: \nL12: Paper 31\nL13: \nL14: ### On-Premises LLM Deployment Demands a Middle Path: Preserving Privacy Without Sacrificing Model\nL15: \nL16: Paper 38\nL17: \nL18: ### In-Context Meta Learning Induces Multi-Phase Circuit Emergence\nL19: \nL20: Paper 87\nL21: ### GASP: Efficient Black-Box Generation of Adversarial Suffixes for Jailbreaking LLMs\nL22: \nL23: ICLR 2025 Workshop - Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOral Presentations 2 - ICLR 2025 Workshop on Trustworthy LLM (https://building-trust-in-llms.github.io/iclr-workshop/oral_presentations_2.html)\nciteturn6904view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn6901view0\",\"id\":6}); Total lines: 24\nL0: # ICLR 2025 Workshop\nL1: Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications:\nL2: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\nL3: \nL4: cite0†HOME cite1†SPEAKERS AND PANELISTS cite2†CALL FOR PAPERS cite3†WORKSHOP SCHEDULE L5: \nL6: ## Oral Presentations 2 (4:20pm-5:00pm)\nL7: \nL8: Paper 43\nL9: \nL10: ### StochasTok: Improving Fine-Grained Subword Understanding in LLMs\nL11: \nL12: Paper 44\nL13: \nL14: ### The Jailbreak Tax: How Useful are Your Jailbreak Outputs?\nL15: \nL16: Paper 75\nL17: \nL18: ### ASIDE: Architectural Separation of Instructions and Data in Language Models\nL19: \nL20: Paper 121\nL21: ### Measuring In-Context Computation Complexity via Hidden State Prediction\nL22: \nL23: ICLR 2025 Workshop - Building Trust in LLMs and LLM Applications: From Guardrails to Explainability to Regulation\n"
